<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">War and march · Celebration · c. 1415 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Agincourt Carol</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>An English song from the early 1400s that tells of King Henry V's win at the Battle of Agincourt and gives thanks to God.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>anonymous</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>English, after Henry V's victory in France</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English (the verses), Latin (the refrain)</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>England; the Trinity Carol Roll probably comes from East Anglia</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>early 15th century</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>War and march · Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (anonymous, early 15th century)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agincourt-carol">Agincourt Carol</a>&quot;, also called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-agincourt">Agincourt</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> or the Agincourt Hymn, is an English song written some time in the early 15th century. It recounts the Battle of Agincourt of 1415, in which the English army led by Henry V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> defeated the army of the French king Charles VI, in what is now the Pas-de-Calais region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p>It survives in two sources of its own time. It is one of thirteen carols on the Trinity Carol Roll, which probably comes from East Anglia and has been held in the Wren Library of Trinity College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, since the 19th century. The other is the Selden Carol Book in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodleian-library">Bodleian Library</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>.</p><p>Its form is that of the religious carols of the period: each verse is sung in English and followed by a refrain, the burden, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>.</p><p>In the 20th century it found new listeners. The composer Ernest Farrar based his Heroic Elegy: For Soldiers of 1918 on it, and Laurence Olivier used it in his film Henry V of 1944. It has been recorded by choirs and folk groups, among them The Young Tradition on the album Galleries, with the Early Music Consort and Dave Swarbrick; the Poxy Boggards on Bawdy Parts; and the Silly Sisters (Maddy Prior and June Tabor) on No More to the Dance.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of thanksgiving for a victory in war. The English verses tell of the king's campaign and battle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> refrain gives the victory to God. The mood is proud and solemn, like a hymn.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">English</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">English</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Deo gratias Anglia redde pro victoria!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>England, give thanks to God for victory!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Owre kynge went forth to Normandy,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Our king went forth to Normandy</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>With grace and myzt of chivalry;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>with grace and might of chivalry;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The God for hym wrouzt marvelously,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>God worked marvellously for him there,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Wherefore Englonde may calle, and cry</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>wherefore England may call and cry:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Deo gratias:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Thanks be to God:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Deo gratias Anglia redde pro victoria.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>England, give thanks to God for victory.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>He sette a sege, the sothe for to say,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He laid a siege, to tell the truth,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>To Harflue toune with ryal aray;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to Harfleur town with royal array;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>That toune he wan, and made a fray,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that town he won, and made a fight</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>That Fraunce shall rywe tyl domes day.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that France shall rue till doomsday.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Then went owre kynge, with alle his oste,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Then went our king, with all his host,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Thorowe Fraunce for all the Frenshe boste;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>through France, for all the French boast;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>He spared ‘for’ drede of leste, ne most,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>he held back for fear of neither least nor greatest</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Tyl he come to Agincourt coste.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>till he came to the Agincourt coast.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Than for sothe that knyzt comely</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Then, in truth, that comely knight</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>In Agincourt seld he sauzt manly,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>fought manfully on Agincourt field;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Thorow grace of God most myzty</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>through the grace of God most mighty</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>He had bothe the felde, and the victory.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>he had both the field and the victory.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Ther dukys, and erlys, lorde and barone,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>There dukes and earls, lord and baron,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Were take, and slayne, and that wel sone,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>were taken and slain, and that right soon,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And some were ledde in to Lundone</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and some were led into London</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>With joye, and merthe, and grete renone.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>with joy and mirth and great renown.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Now gracious God he save owre kynge,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Now gracious God, may he save our king,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>His peple, and all his wel wyllynge,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>his people, and all who wish him well;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Gef him gode lyfe, and gode endynge,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>give him good life and a good ending,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>That we with merth mowe savely synge</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that we with mirth may safely sing:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Deo gratias:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Thanks be to God:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Deo gratias Anglia redde pro victoria.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>England, give thanks to God for victory.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Anonymous (15th century), as printed in Thomas Percy's Reliques of Ancient English Poetry (1765). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Reliques_of_Ancient_English_Poetry%2FVolume_2%2FFor_the_Victory_at_Agincourt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (3)</summary><ul><li>A plain modern rendering of the 15th-century English, made line by line from Percy’s text.</li><li>Percy prints z for the old letter yogh (myzt = might, wrouzt = wrought) and &quot;seld he sauzt&quot; for &quot;feld he faught&quot; (field he fought).</li><li>In the manuscript the Latin refrain follows every stanza; Percy marks it &quot;Deo gratias, &amp;c.&quot;, left out here.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
